$string['promptpublishinstructions'] = 'After saving the draft, present a preview and offer two actions in the conversation: Approve and Reject (do not embed these in the HTML). Publish only after the teacher explicitly approves. If rejected, keep the draft unpublished and ask for the requested changes.';
$string['promptsurvey'] = 'Using the MCP connection to {$a->wwwroot}, in Moodle course {$a->courseid}, edit only the EXISTING mod_mcp activity "{$a->name}" (course-module ID {$a->cmid}, URL {$a->url}). Build a SATISFACTION SURVEY, not a graded quiz. Ask the teacher about its purpose, audience, number of questions, scales/response types, required answers and any need for reports or anonymity. Display exactly ONE question at a time. For single-choice answers, immediately prevent duplicate selections, persist the answer and ONLY after a successful save animate the selected question out (for example opacity and translation) and the next one in; do not require a Next button for single-choice answers. For free text or multi-select use a clear confirmation button before advancing. Respect prefers-reduced-motion, keyboard navigation, focus, screen readers, validation and save-error states; never advance or lose an answer when persistence fails. Show progress, allow resuming after reloading, and display a final confirmation. Use MoodleMCP.progress.load()/save({version, location, data}) to persist per-learner answers and position; if also updating structured progress using progress.saveStatus(), use the version returned by the preceding save and never issue concurrent writes. Respect API storage limits and do not place unnecessary personal data into tracking events. The current API has no consolidated teacher response reporting and does not guarantee anonymity: if these are required, explain that backend support is necessary instead of promising them using HTML/JavaScript alone. Do not create graded questions, answer keys or official grades.';
$string['prompttimeline'] = 'Using the MCP connection to {$a->wwwroot}, in Moodle course {$a->courseid}, edit only the EXISTING mod_mcp activity "{$a->name}" (course-module ID {$a->cmid}, URL {$a->url}). Build an explorable educational TIMELINE. Ask the teacher for the subject, timeframe, key events, audience and display preferences. Arrange events in verified chronological order with date/period, title, description and sources when provided; never fabricate dates or facts. Provide responsive desktop and mobile views with expandable events, accessible keyboard navigation, sufficient contrast and readable text. Add period or category filters when useful. Allow revisiting events without losing state, and save the learner position with MoodleMCP.progress.getStatus()/saveStatus() when the timeline has sequential steps, respecting versions and conflicts. Reading progress is not native Moodle completion. Generate a complete HTML document with appropriate CSS and JavaScript, without a quiz or grades by default.';
$string['promptcase'] = 'Using the MCP connection to {$a->wwwroot}, in Moodle course {$a->courseid}, edit only the EXISTING mod_mcp activity "{$a->name}" (course-module ID {$a->cmid}, URL {$a->url}). Build a DECISION-BASED CASE STUDY WITH CONSEQUENCES. Ask the teacher for the scenario, audience, learning objective, factual context, approximate number of decision points and whether choices can be revisited. Present the initial situation and ONE decision per step with plausible alternatives, distinct branches, coherent consequences and explanatory feedback. Include both successful and unsuccessful outcomes without dead ends; allow revisiting the decision path and retrying other choices when appropriate. Model a finite, consistent decision graph; verify all possible paths and avoid references to nonexistent steps. Save the individual step, selections and decision path using MoodleMCP.progress.load()/save() with version checks and conflict handling; update percentage via progress.saveStatus() sequentially when useful. Do not call the simulation an official graded assessment: if grading is requested, use only Moodle-supported native questions and server-side grading, never a score computed inside the iframe. Produce complete, accessible, responsive HTML.';
$string['promptsimulation'] = 'Using the MCP connection to {$a->wwwroot}, in Moodle course {$a->courseid}, edit only the EXISTING mod_mcp activity "{$a->name}" (course-module ID {$a->cmid}, URL {$a->url}). Build an INTERACTIVE SIMULATION in which learners change parameters and observe outcomes. Ask the teacher for the phenomenon or process, audience, input parameters, units, rules or formulas, safe bounds and expected outputs. Include accessible inputs (fields, selectors or sliders), current values, outputs and helpful graphs or diagrams, presets, a reset control and explanations of each change. Validate inputs and bounds, keep units consistent, handle invalid states and avoid ungrounded models; ask the teacher for trustworthy assumptions if reliable formulas are missing before implementing. Clearly label simplifications and assumptions and never present the simulation as real-world measurement. Save scenarios and parameters with MoodleMCP.progress.load()/save() and version conflict handling; for guided sequences, use progress.getStatus()/saveStatus() with fresh versions. Never assign official grades based on HTML calculations. Generate complete, accessible, responsive HTML, CSS and JavaScript.';
$string['promptcustom'] = 'Using the MCP connection to {$a->wwwroot}, in Moodle course {$a->courseid}, edit only the EXISTING mod_mcp activity "{$a->name}" (course-module ID {$a->cmid}, URL {$a->url}). This is the I CHOOSE mode: the FIRST ChatGPT or Claude response must ask the teacher directly "What kind of activity would you like to create?" and invite unrestricted free-text directions describing the experience, its content and its learning objective. Examples may inspire the teacher, but never limit the answer to predefined choices or assume a quiz, page or other format. Ask whether there are documents or reference materials, and WAIT for the responses before planning, generating content or calling write tools. Then clarify only the requirements relevant to the chosen format and create a complete HTML experience, with CSS and JavaScript when useful, following the documented mod_mcp APIs. If a graded assessment is requested, use native Moodle question and grading services, never client-side grading. Preserve teacher freedom while respecting security, privacy, accessibility, progress and the activity-wide instructions.';
